--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1) Directly adapted (code)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  app/audio/kokoro_tts.py
      Kokoro local neural TTS backend. Adapted from OpenJarvis
      src/openjarvis/speech/kokoro_tts.py
      https://github.com/open-jarvis/OpenJarvis/blob/main/src/openjarvis/speech/kokoro_tts.py
      Changes: removed OpenJarvis's TTSRegistry decorator and TTSBackend/TTSResult
      abstraction; the pipeline is lazily import-guarded; synthesis returns raw
      float32 samples (played by app/audio/text_to_speech.py through sounddevice)
      instead of encoded audio bytes.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2) Patterns adapted (reimplemented — no code copied)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  app/brain/planning.py
      Compact, self-contained reimplementation of three ideas from OpenJarvis.
      No source code was copied; only the approaches were studied.
        - Explicit up-front task decomposition before acting, inspired by
          src/openjarvis/agents/manager.py and src/openjarvis/agents/executor.py
        - Defensive JSON-or-lines parsing of model output that never raises,
          inspired by src/openjarvis/memory/extractor.py (FactExtractor)
        - Token-budget context compaction that elides stale tool observations,
          inspired by src/openjarvis/agents/hybrid/mini_swe_agent.py
          (_estimate_prompt_tokens and _compact_local_messages)

  app/brain/agent.py (partial)
      The plan -> select-tool -> act -> observe loop with a step budget and the
      context-compaction hook are pattern-inspired by the OpenJarvis agent files
      above. JARVIS's own safety model (AGENT_ALLOWED_DIRS allowlist, per-action
      approval prompts, untrusted-content handling) is unchanged.

================================================================================
Notes
================================================================================

- Speech-to-text (app/audio/speech_to_text.py) uses faster-whisper, the same
  engine OpenJarvis wraps. No OpenJarvis code was used; parity was confirmed and
  no second STT stack was added.
- The paid/cloud TTS/STT backends OpenJarvis also ships (Cartesia, Deepgram,
  OpenAI) were deliberately NOT adapted — JARVIS stays local and free.
